  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe bottom end of the upper trough will be swinging over the \nsouthern Appalachians today on its way to its rendezvous with the \ncoastal storm. This should help initiate some additional rain \nshowers, mainly in the morning hours but cannot rule out an isolated \nshower in the afternoon. Cannot rule out thunder with today's \nactivity, but if any bark occurs not expecting a bite. Next few \nmornings starting Friday morning will be pleasantly cool, and a few \nlucky souls in southwest VA or far northeastern TN may see \ntemperatures dip below 40F! For example, there's a 30% chance of \nthis occurring per the NBM at both Mountain City and Glade Spring. \n\nTrough quickly ejects offshore as we head into the weekend and \nbackside flow means we should be dry, pleasantly warm but \ncomfortable with low afternoon RH values, and enjoying a good \nentrance into normal early fall weather for the MidSouth. Rain \nchances don't look to return until next Wednesday when both major \nmodels agree that a classic fall longwave trough will march from \nwest to east across the CONUS. GFS is doing its usual too speedy \nbias, but NBM increases rain chances Wednesday to end the forecast \nand the month of September.",
